WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.238
Why this matters.

2
00:00:01.238 --> 00:00:05.237
Almost every network connection
begins with a DNS query,

3
00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:10.938
and in a hybrid multi-cloud agency the
answer now decides far more than an address.

4
00:00:10.938 --> 00:00:16.442
NIST's 2026 DNS deployment guide,
SP 800-81 Revision 3, says DNS

5
00:00:16.442 --> 00:00:23.376
infrastructure has become more than a
service; it is also a security control.

6
00:00:23.376 --> 00:00:27.525
Chapter 01 names six jobs DNS now does.

7
00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:32.279
It publishes private addresses: an Azure
Storage account with a private endpoint

8
00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:37.312
resolves privately inside the hosting
virtual network and publicly everywhere else.

9
00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:43.179
It selects SaaS front doors: Microsoft
365 publishes a Worldwide list,

10
00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:49.349
which includes GCC, and separate lists
and namespaces for GCC High and DoD.

11
00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:54.837
It enforces security policy through
response policy zones and DNS firewalls.

12
00:00:54.837 --> 00:01:00.624
It anchors certificate validation,
because OCSP and CRL endpoints are names.

13
00:01:00.624 --> 00:01:03.948
And the FQDN is the service
identity that gateways,

14
00:01:03.948 --> 00:01:07.825
certificates and Zero Trust
policies are written against.

15
00:01:07.825 --> 00:01:12.625
Yet most agencies run it on as many as
five platforms that share no state.

16
00:01:12.625 --> 00:01:16.475
AD DNS or a DDI grid on premises.

17
00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:21.700
Route 53's VPC Resolver,
formerly Route 53 Resolver.

18
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.075
Azure, where private zones
resolve only for linked networks,

19
00:01:26.075 --> 00:01:30.275
and where DNS resolver policy
now gives an RPZ equivalent.

20
00:01:30.275 --> 00:01:32.350
Google Cloud's metadata server.

21
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.625
And the NSX forwarder, which
workloads reach only if configured to.

22
00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:39.150
The cost shows up on every change.

23
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.589
A new Azure private endpoint
can need four more DNS changes,

24
00:01:43.589 --> 00:01:46.125
on four consoles, in four syntaxes.

25
00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:49.838
Chapter 09 assigns ownership,
because the forwarding rules

26
00:01:49.838 --> 00:01:53.620
that connect the platforms
usually belong to no single team.
